Persistent console log on the eMMC root + scheduled self-reboot (TWRP loop).

In the `fastboot boot` mode the panel stays dark, so the kernel console must go
to flash.  Patch 0047 already keeps every console byte (kernel printf and
/dev/console writes) in a 4 MiB ring and flushes it from the UFS driver; on
this port the root is eMMC, so this patch adds the eMMC flusher.  The on-flash
format is unchanged, so tools/sweet-log-pull.sh + tools/sweet-log.py decode it:

  block 0:        "SWEETLOG" | u32 version 1 | u32 ring size |
                  u64 total bytes | u64 flush seq
  blocks 1..1024: the 4 MiB ring (console byte n at ring offset n % size)

stored in the LAST 4 MiB + 4 KiB (1025 x 4 KiB blocks = 8200 x 512-byte LBAs)
of the selected eMMC partition - far outside the 20 GiB HFS image, so the file
system never touches it.  The flush thread reads the existing 0047 ring via
sweet_log_position()/sweet_log_copy() and writes whole 4 KiB blocks with
oe_write_lba() under onc_emmc_lock.

Boot args (all optional):
  sweetlog=0             disable the eMMC console log (default: enabled, like
                         0047; only runs when emmcroot_orw=1 made the card
                         writable)
  emcmlogsecs=<secs>     flush interval (default 10, clamped 1..60)
  sweetrebootat=<secs>   arm a self-reboot after <secs> seconds (default off);
                         prints "SWEET REBOOT: armed for N s" then
                         "SWEET REBOOT: firing now" and calls
                         reboot_kernel(RB_AUTOBOOT, "sweetrebootat")

A banner with the flash LBA is printed when the log thread starts:
  SWEET LOG: console log to the last 4100 KiB of the partition (block 0x...)

Only bsd/dev/onc_emmc.c changes.
